      "problem": "${2}^{-3}\\times (\\frac{8}{5}+4)=${{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "To evaluate the given expression, we use PEMDAS.\n\nNotice that ${2}^{-3}=\\frac{1}{{2}^{3}}.$ So, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{-3}\\times (\\frac{8}{5}+4) & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{{2}^{3}}\\times (\\frac{8}{5}+4) & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{8}\\times (\\frac{8}{5}+4) & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{8}\\times \\frac{8+20}{5} & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{8}\\times \\frac{28}{5} & = \\\\ \\frac{1\\times 28}{8\\times 5} & = \\\\ \\frac{1\\times 4\\times 7}{2\\times 4\\times 5} & = \\\\ \\frac{1\\times \\cancel{4}\\times 7}{2\\times \\cancel{4}\\times 5} & = \\\\ \\frac{7}{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
